==Finnish==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|non|fi}} {{m|non|herra}}, from {{etyl|osx|fi}} {{m|osx|hērro}}, from {{etyl|goh|fi}} {{m|odt|hēriro}}, {{m|goh|hērro}}, the comparative form of {{m|goh|hēr||noble, venerable}} (German {{m|de|hehr}}), by analogy with Latin {{m|la|senior||elder}}. Cognates include Danish, Norwegian and Swedish {{m|sv|herre}}, Icelandic {{m|is|herra}}, Dutch {{m|nl|heer}}, German {{m|de|Herr}}. The Old High German word originally meant "grey, grey-haired", and descends from {{etyl|gem-pro|fi}} {{m|gem-pro|*hairaz||grey}}, making it cognate with Old English {{m|ang|hār}} (English {{m|en|hoar}}), Old Norse {{m|non|hárr}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|Fi-herra.ogg|Audio|lang=fi}}
* {{hyphenation|her|ra|lang=fi}}
* {{rhymes|lang=fi|erːɑ}}
* {{IPA|lang=fi|/ˈherːɑ/}}

===Noun===
{{fi-noun}}

# [[mister]], [[gentleman]], [[sir]] (polite address)
#: ''Anteeksi, '''herra''' ...''
#:: Excuse me, '''sir''' ...
#: ''Hyvät '''herrat'''!''
#:: '''Gentlemen'''!
#: '''''Herra''' Virtanen, voinko puhua kanssanne?''
#:: '''Mister''' Virtanen, may I speak with you?
# [[lord]] (person having formal authority over others)
# lord (person enjoying great respect in a community)
# lord, [[master]] (owner)
# capitalized ({{m|fi|Herra}}), [[Lord]] (God)

