==Icelandic==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|non|is}} {{m|non|herra}}, from {{etyl|osx|is}} {{m|osx|hērro}}, from {{etyl|goh|is}} {{m|odt|hēriro}}, {{m|goh|hērro}}, the comparative form of {{m|goh|hēr||noble, venerable}} (German {{m|de|hehr}}), by analogy with Latin {{m|la|senior||elder}}. Cognates include Danish, Norwegian and Swedish {{m|sv|herre}}, Dutch {{m|nl|heer}}, German {{m|de|Herr}}. The Old High German word originally meant "grey, grey-haired", and descends from {{etyl|gem-pro|is}} {{m|gem-pro|*hairaz||grey}}, making it cognate with Old English {{m|ang|hār}} (English {{m|en|hoar}}), Old Norse {{m|non|hárr}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈhɛrːa/|lang=is}}
*: {{rhymes|ɛrːa|lang=is}}

===Noun===
{{is-noun|m|herra|herrar}}

# [[lord]], [[master]]
# the [[Lord]] (God)
# [[mister]], [[gentleman]]
# [[sir]]

====Declension====
{{is-decl-noun-m-w1a|h|e|rr}}

====Usage notes====
Used as a title (prenominally or on its own) for bishops and for the president of Iceland (also, especially historically, for some other dignitaries). Also used as a general courtesy title for men, equivalent to English {{m|en|mister}}, but then usually only in formal written correspondence and abbreviated {{m|is|Hr.}} However, when it is used as an honorary title for bishops, presidents, etc., it should never be abbreviated. The equivalent female title is {{m|is|frú}} (in both contexts).

====See also====
* {{label|is|archaic}} [[herri]]

